PRIVATE REFLECTION: {Split persists (now ~equal A vs C). Prior lean on C reinforced by Colleagues 2/3/4/10/11/12: residual light-dependent labeling requires sensitized retro-[2+2] of bicyclo[4.2.0] core (common to both probes), releasing electrophilic Michael acceptor C that alkylates proteins and supplies the alkyne for post-irradiation Cy5 click. Phenol enables dominant extra pathway (B) explaining much stronger first-probe signal. 
Counter-arguments for A (Colleagues 1/5/6/7/8/9/13/14): only 417 nm absorber is thioxanthenone, so it must initiate any observable light-vs-dark difference via direct H-abstraction/ET from benzylic OH or protein; C would not produce strictly light-dependent labeling once formed and lacks experimental support (no fragmentation evidence). B/D/E eliminated for second probe (no phenol, timing, structure). 
Unresolved tension: whether sensitizer acts solely by enabling ring-opening (C) or by direct probe oxidation (A). No new data on wavelength dependence or trapping. My updated lean still C for probe-specific residual difference, but A remains viable if fragmentation efficiency is low.}

